\description{
\code{apa_table()} turns a tidy table (or a fitted model) into the tibble
that \code{\link[apa7:apa_flextable]{apa7::apa_flextable()}} renders in an apaquarto manuscript: every
value already formatted as text, every header already its APA
markdown, and the text of the table note attached for \code{\link[=apa_note]{apa_note()}}.
It is the table counterpart of \code{\link[=apa_inline]{apa_inline()}} and takes the same
formatting options.
}
\section{Scope}{

\code{apa_table()} targets a model-parameter table: one row per parameter
(or per hypothesis, model or pair, depending on the contract type),
one column per statistic. It has no pivot and no second estimate
column, so a table shaped by a design factor — one row per
item-feature cell, one column per measurement occasion — or one that
lays two samples' estimates side by side is outside it. Carry its
tibble on into your own reshaping (\code{tidyr::pivot_wider()} and the
like) and then into \code{\link[apa7:apa_flextable]{apa7::apa_flextable()}}; the vignette "Reporting
brms models in apaquarto" has a worked example of exactly that,
\code{apa_table()} output pivoted into a cross-tabulated design table.
}

\section{What is printed}{

A \code{parameters} table has one row per parameter: the label column
(\code{Path} when every term is a structural-equation path, else
\code{Predictor}), the estimate (\verb{*Mdn*} for a posterior median, \verb{*M*} for a
posterior mean, \code{Estimate} otherwise), the interval, and then, in this
order, whichever of \verb{*pd*}, \verb{\% in ROPE}, \verb{*BF*~10~} and \verb{*p*} the
table carries. \verb{*R̂*}, \verb{ESS~bulk~} and \verb{ESS~tail~} are added with
\code{stats}. The interval header reads \verb{95\% CrI}, \verb{95\% HDI} or \verb{95\% CI},
from the rows' own \code{ci_method} and \code{ci_level}; rows that disagree on
the level carry it in their cells (\verb{90\% [0.12, 0.48]}), and rows that
disagree on the method carry their label (\code{HDI [0.12, 0.48]}) under
the header \verb{95\% Interval} (\code{Interval} when the levels differ too). A
bare \code{CI} label, which apa7 would format as a column of its own, moves
into the cells the same way, and a \code{ci_label} that apa7 would format
is refused. A \code{diagnostics} table from
\code{\link[=apa_tidy_diagnostics]{apa_tidy_diagnostics()}} has a \code{Term} column and \verb{*R̂*}, \verb{ESS~bulk~}
and \verb{ESS~tail~}.

A \code{hypotheses} table from \code{\link[brms:hypothesis]{brms::hypothesis()}} has one row per
hypothesis: \code{Hypothesis}, \code{Group} when some row has one, the estimate
and its interval as on a parameters table (rows tested at different
levels carry the level in their cells), and \verb{*BF*~10~}; \code{stats} adds
the evidence ratio \code{ER} and the posterior probability \verb{*P*(H)}. A
\code{loo} table from \code{\link[loo:loo_compare]{loo::loo_compare()}} has \code{Model}, the difference to
the first model with its standard error in the cell, \verb{ΔELPD (*SE*)},
the model's own \verb{ELPD (*SE*)}, \verb{*p*~loo~}, and \verb{*w*} when the table
was extracted with \verb{weights =}; \code{LOOIC} is added with \code{stats}. A
\code{bf_models} table has \code{Model}, \verb{*BF*~10~} against the denominator
model (which prints \code{1.00}) and, from the BayesFactor route, the
proportional error \verb{Error (\%)}; \code{stats} adds \verb{log(*BF*~10~)} and the
posterior model probability \verb{*P*(M | D)}. A \code{bf_inclusion} table from
\code{\link[bayestestR:bayesfactor_inclusion]{bayestestR::bayesfactor_inclusion()}} has \code{Term}, \verb{*P*(incl)},
\verb{*P*(incl | D)} and \verb{*BF*~incl~} (\verb{*BF*~excl~} under
\code{bf_direction = "01"}). A Bayes factor too large or too small to print
leaves its cell empty and is given in a log column instead; an
inclusion Bayes factor that is missing or infinite leaves its cell
empty. The note says which, and why.

Every number goes through the format layer (\code{\link[=apa_num]{apa_num()}}, \code{\link[=apa_pd]{apa_pd()}},
\code{\link[=apa_prob]{apa_prob()}}, \code{\link[=apa_bf]{apa_bf()}}, \code{\link[=apa_er]{apa_er()}}, \code{\link[=apa_p]{apa_p()}}) and is decimal-aligned
with \code{\link[apa7:align_chr]{apa7::align_chr()}}, except Bayes factors and evidence ratios,
whose markup and bounds the alignment would count as digits. A missing
value is an empty cell. The ROPE share and the error drop their \verb{\%},
which the header carries. No header equals a column name apa7 formats
itself, so \code{apa_flextable()} renders the table as it is.

The note defines the abbreviations the table shows (\verb{*Mdn* = posterior median; CrI = equal-tailed credible interval; *pd* = probability of direction.}), names the reference model of a comparison and the
denominator of a Bayes factor, states that the estimates are
standardized when every row is, and on a diagnostics table counts the
divergent transitions. Nothing printed judges the result.

A \code{sem_fit} table has one row per model: \code{Model} when any row names
one, then \verb{*χ*^2^}, \verb{*df*}, \verb{*p*} and the fit indices the table
records, each index whose interval it records carrying that interval
in the cell (\verb{RMSEA [90\% CI]}, \code{.092 [.071, .114]}). A \code{contrasts}
table has \code{Contrast}, \code{Group} when the contrasts were computed within
one, the estimate and its interval, \verb{*pd*} and \verb{\% in ROPE}. A
\code{correlations} table names each pair in \verb{Variable 1} and \verb{Variable 2},
then \verb{*r*}, its interval, \verb{*pd*}, \verb{*BF*~10~} and the pairwise \verb{*n*},
which can differ between rows.

A frequentist interval is headed \verb{95\% CI (Wald)} or
\verb{95\% CI (bootstrap)} rather than \verb{95\% CI}, which apa7 reads as a
column of its own to format; \code{\link[=apa_inline]{apa_inline()}} still writes \verb{95\% CI} in
running text.
}

\section{Rendering in apaquarto}{

Make the table in an earlier chunk, then name the note in the chunk
option \code{apa-note} of the chunk that renders it:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{```\{r\}
tab <- apa_table(fit)
```

```\{r\}
#| label: tbl-fit
#| tbl-cap: Posterior summary of the regression
#| apa-note: !expr apa_note(tab)
apa7::apa_flextable(tab)
```
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The two chunks are needed because knitr evaluates an \code{!expr} chunk
option before it runs the chunk, so a table made in the same chunk does
not exist yet when the note is read. \code{apa_note()} refuses a table with
nothing to define rather than return \code{NULL} or \code{""}, because apaquarto
renders either as an empty note, \verb{Note. \{\}}; remove the \code{apa-note}
option for such a table. \code{apa_flextable()} drops the attributes of the
tibble, so the note is read from the tibble, never from the flextable.
}

\section{papaja}{

papaja also exports a function called \code{apa_table()}. Whichever of the
two packages is attached last masks the other's. With both attached,
call \code{apabayes::apa_table()}.
}

\section{The default method}{

On a fitted model or any other object \code{\link[=apa_tidy]{apa_tidy()}} accepts, the default
method calls \code{apa_tidy(x, ...)} and tabulates the result, so \code{...}
takes that route's arguments: \code{standardize = TRUE} on a lavaan or
blavaan fit, \code{effects = "all"} on a brms fit, \code{ci = "hdi"} or \verb{rope =}
on any posterior.
}

\examples{
t <- apabayes_tidy(
  data.frame(
    term = c("b_Intercept", "b_wt", "sigma"),
    label = c("(Intercept)", "wt", "sigma"),
    estimate = c(37.3, -5.34, 2.71), ci_low = c(31.2, -6.85, 2.09),
    ci_high = c(43.1, -3.82, 3.60), pd = c(1, 0.9995, 1),
    rhat = c(1.001, 1.002, 1.004), ess_bulk = c(1520, 1633, 2401),
    component = c("conditional", "conditional", "sigma")
  ),
  type = "parameters", centrality = "median", ci_method = "eti",
  ci_level = 0.95
)
tab <- apa_table(t)
tab
apa_note(tab)
apa_table(t, stats = c("pd", "rhat", "ess_bulk"), group_rows = TRUE)

# A model comparison: the standard error travels in the cell, and the
# note names the model the differences are taken from.
cmp <- apabayes_tidy(
  data.frame(
    model = c("full", "additive", "null"),
    elpd_diff = c(0, -1.83, -24.6), se_diff = c(0, 1.85, 6.44),
    elpd = c(-56.51, -58.34, -81.11), se_elpd = c(4.07, 4.48, 6.12),
    p_loo = c(3.51, 2.50, 1.24)
  ),
  type = "loo", reference = "full"
)
apa_table(cmp)
apa_note(apa_table(cmp))

# A Bayes factor too large to print keeps its row: the cell is empty,
# the log column comes in unasked, and the note says why.
bfm <- apabayes_tidy(
  data.frame(
    model = c("intercept only", "wt", "wt + hp"),
    bf = c(1, 4.6e7, Inf), log_bf = c(0, 17.64, 779.19),
    denominator = c(TRUE, FALSE, FALSE)
  ),
  type = "bf_models", denominator_model = "intercept only"
)
apa_table(bfm)
apa_note(apa_table(bfm))

# Fit indices: one row per model, each index carrying its interval in
# the cell.
fits <- apabayes_tidy(
  data.frame(
    model = c("One factor", "Two factors"),
    chisq = c(85.31, 24.02), df = c(24, 19), p = c(8.5e-09, 0.196),
    cfi = c(0.931, 0.994), tli = c(0.896, 0.991),
    rmsea = c(0.092, 0.030), rmsea_low = c(0.071, 0.000),
    rmsea_high = c(0.114, 0.061), rmsea_level = c(0.90, 0.90),
    srmr = c(0.065, 0.031)
  ),
  type = "sem_fit", centrality = NA, ci_method = NA, ci_level = NA
)
apa_table(fits)
apa_note(apa_table(fits))

# Correlations: each pair named in two columns, with the pairwise n.
cors <- apabayes_tidy(
  data.frame(
    term = c("mpg~~wt", "mpg~~hp"), var1 = c("mpg", "mpg"),
    var2 = c("wt", "hp"), estimate = c(-0.82, -0.70),
    ci_low = c(-0.92, -0.84), ci_high = c(-0.66, -0.51),
    pd = c(1, 1), bf = c(1.3e7, 3.9e4), n = c(32, 32),
    method = "Bayesian Pearson correlation"
  ),
  type = "correlations", centrality = "median", ci_method = "hdi",
  ci_level = 0.95
)
apa_table(cors)
apa_note(apa_table(cors))
\dontshow{if (rlang::is_installed("lavaan")) withAutoprint(\{ # examplesIf}
hs <- lavaan::HolzingerSwineford1939
fit <- lavaan::cfa("visual =~ x1 + x2 + x3", hs)
apa_table(fit, standardize = TRUE)
\dontshow{\}) # examplesIf}
}
